WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.700
Read a trophic hormone with its target hormone, and the pair points to the failing

2
00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:11.020
link. Now take a healthy adult whose TSH reads low and free T4 reads high.

3
00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:14.280
That pattern says overactive thyroid.

4
00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:16.660
So where did it come from?

5
00:00:20.067 --> 00:00:22.827
The answer lies in how each number was made.

6
00:00:24.047 --> 00:00:28.507
For TSH, a large peptide, two antibodies grip one molecule.

7
00:00:29.787 --> 00:00:32.347
One holds it down, the other carries a signal.

8
00:00:33.627 --> 00:00:35.007
More hormone, more signal.

9
00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:41.640
Free T4 is too small for two antibodies at once, so it is usually measured

10
00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:47.760
competitively. Sample hormone and a labelled copy compete for a limited antibody.

11
00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:53.920
More sample hormone, less label bound, so signal falls as hormone rises.

12
00:00:55.050 --> 00:00:59.790
Some assays catch the antibody complex with biotin and streptavidin.

13
00:01:00.850 --> 00:01:03.190
A biotin supplement can block that catch.

14
00:01:04.490 --> 00:01:08.230
Less complex is captured, so the signal falls in both assays.

15
00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:12.400
Now each assay turns signal into a concentration.

16
00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:17.100
In the sandwich assay, less signal reads as less TSH.

17
00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:22.480
In the competitive assay, less signal reads as more free T4.

18
00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:26.940
Together they draw an overactive thyroid that is not there.

19
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:32.600
In a 2020 study, ten healthy adults took biotin daily for a week.

20
00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:40.200
Two hours after the last dose, one platform read lower TSH and higher free T4 than

21
00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:45.840
baseline. Another platform's tested assays showed no significant change.

22
00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:48.400
Here is the one idea.

23
00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:52.220
A hormone number is what the assay detected.

24
00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:57.100
That is not always what the blood holds, or what a receptor sees.

25
00:01:58.460 --> 00:02:01.400
So before you read a pair, ask what the assay measured.

26
00:02:02.483 --> 00:02:06.023
Nearly all T4 in blood rides on carrier proteins.

27
00:02:07.423 --> 00:02:10.303
Only the free part is considered available to tissues.

28
00:02:12.003 --> 00:02:16.723
Oral estrogen raises thyroxine-binding globulin, so total T4 rises.

29
00:02:18.123 --> 00:02:20.303
What do free T4 and TSH do?

30
00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:30.830
Free T4 stays near its usual range, and TSH stays normal.

31
00:02:32.290 --> 00:02:35.630
Pair TSH with total T4, and the rule seems broken.

32
00:02:36.970 --> 00:02:39.250
Pair it with free T4, and the rule holds.

33
00:02:40.283 --> 00:02:41.823
Back to our healthy adult.

34
00:02:43.263 --> 00:02:46.443
Rerun the same blood on a platform without a biotin step.

35
00:02:47.763 --> 00:02:52.023
A real change should show on both platforms, an artefact only on one.

36
00:02:53.283 --> 00:02:57.403
The pair still points to the failing link, once you know how each number was made.
